Subject: [PATCH] tun: support declarative default interface priority on Windows

Problem:
On multi-homed Windows hosts using Automatic Metric, two or more active
adapters (such as Wi-Fi and a USB/dock Ethernet adapter) frequently have
0.0.0.0/0 default routes whose combined metrics (row.Metric + iface.Metric)
are equal or within a few points of each other. Minor Wi-Fi link-speed
changes or virtual switch events (for example Hyper-V VmSwitch) recompute
the effective metric and cause sing-tun's DefaultInterfaceMonitor to flip
repeatedly between interfaces, breaking connections on each switch.
In addition, when two interfaces tie on metric, upstream checkUpdate()
relied on OS route table iteration order instead of keeping the active
interface, while hand-tuning OS interface metrics is opaque and brittle.

Solution:
- Add PreferredInterfaces []string to DefaultInterfaceMonitorOptions and
  store a filtered copy on defaultInterfaceMonitor.
- Extract selectDefaultInterface() in monitor_priority.go:
  1. When PreferredInterfaces is non-empty, select the first configured
     interface alias that is currently connected and holds a default
     route, regardless of metric differences or jitter.
  2. When the primary interface disconnects, automatically fail over to
     the next available preferred interface, and fail back when the
     primary returns.
  3. When PreferredInterfaces is empty or none of its interfaces are
     available, fall back to the lowest combined metric (with per-index
     deduplication in monitor_windows.go) and break equal-metric ties in
     favor of the currently active default interface.
- Preserve full backward compatibility for callers omitting
  PreferredInterfaces.
- Add unit tests in monitor_priority_test.go covering priority override,
  failover, failback, fallback to metric, tie-break stickiness, and
  empty candidate lists.

---
